DISCUSSION
The description hands us the whole attack. With e=3 and no padding, K is drawn from the interval N^(1/3) < K < (2N)^(1/3), so K^3 lands strictly between N and 2N. Since the ciphertext Y = K^3 mod N is smaller than N, there is no modular reduction at all in effect: Y + N = K^3 exactly. That makes K just the integer cube root of (Y + N), recoverable with `gmpy2.iroot`.

Once K is known, Y = K^3 − N, and re-encoding Y as a big-endian 192-byte octet string gives us back the exact keystream that was XORed with the secret: C = Y_os XOR S, so S = C XOR Y_os. `deliverables/file.enc` is C, `deliverables/key.enc` is Y_os (192 bytes), and `deliverables/pubkey.pem` gives N (e=3). The recovered S is the 64-byte secret containing the flag.

